    "case_law": {
      "jurisdiction": "Montana state courts",
      "method": "v1.1.0 case-law rebuild: authorities selected per decision point, each opinion retrieved from CourtListener and read; holding and quote taken from the opinion text; independently re-verified before release. Previous keyword-matched Justia listings were removed.",
      "verified_date": "2026-09-20",
      "key_cases": [
        {
          "case_name": "In re J.H.",
          "citation": "2016 MT 35, 382 Mont. 214, 367 P.3d 339",
          "court": "Montana Supreme Court",
          "year": 2016,
          "date_decided": "2016-02-16",
          "holding": "The court affirmed approval of a permanency plan and an award of long-term custody until the child reached 18: the Department's ICPC-based efforts to place the child with his out-of-state father were reasonable, and placement with the long-term caregiver alongside his sibling served the child's best interests.",
          "relevance": "It applies § 41-3-445's planned permanent living arrangement, granting long-term custody until the youth turns 18 — the nearest published Montana authority to extended foster care.",
          "quote": "Rather, “the child's health and safety are of paramount concern,” when determining what efforts are required.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8072335/in-re-jh/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 8072335
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re J.S.",
          "citation": "2014 MT 79, 374 Mont. 329, 321 P.3d 103",
          "court": "Montana Supreme Court",
          "year": 2014,
          "date_decided": "2014-03-25",
          "holding": "A § 41-3-444 guardianship of an Indian child with his long-term foster family was affirmed: early ICWA notice defects were cured by the father's and the tribe's later participation, and 25 U.S.C. § 1912(d) and (e) did not defeat the guardianship where the father never had custody of the child.",
          "relevance": "For a teenager who has spent years in care, this case shows guardianship operating as the permanency alternative to termination and adoption.",
          "quote": "Under Montana law, a guardian may be appointed for a child who has been placed in the custody of the Department during a child abuse or neglect proceeding.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8072121/in-re-js/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 8072121
        },
        {
          "case_name": "In re A.J.W.",
          "citation": "2010 MT 42, 355 Mont. 264, 227 P.3d 1012",
          "court": "Montana Supreme Court",
          "year": 2010,
          "date_decided": "2010-03-02",
          "holding": "Termination was affirmed; counsel was not ineffective for failing to move for long-term custody under § 41-3-445 or guardianship under § 41-3-444, because a parent may not petition for a guardian, the Department must consent, and there was virtually no chance the alternative would have been granted, so no prejudice was shown.",
          "relevance": "It identifies long-term custody under § 41-3-445 as the statutory alternative to termination for an older youth and explains why counsel was not required to pursue it here.",
          "quote": "Even if there were ineffective representation, it is inconsequential unless the parent suffered prejudice as a result.",
          "url": "https://www.courtlistener.com/opinion/8071813/in-re-ajw/",
          "courtlistener_cluster_id": 8071813
        }
      ],
